## Extras in Writeout, Bitbook and OpenDB

* **Math**: `$x^2$` writes math in a line, like $x^2 + y^2 = r^2$, and `$$ ... $$` gives a centered equation. An equation box with buttons helps you build it without knowing LaTeX. Double-click an equation anywhere (a Svelte component that only shows text, or a Plume piece too) and the same box opens to edit it; the new LaTeX is written back into the text it came from.
* **Footnotes**: `[^1]` adds a numbered note, and a line starting with `[^1]:` gives its text.
* **Links**: in OpenDB, `[[entry-name]]` links to another entry, and `[[entry-name|other words]]` changes the words shown.
* **Templates**: in OpenDB, notices like `{{stub}}` and `{{citation needed}}` mark entries that need work. See the [Templates](#/templates) page.

## Keydown Next

The newest step for Keydown: three things at once, working everywhere Keydown works (Writeout, Bitbook, Amber, OpenDB, Keydown.js and the Svelte components).

### Custom tags

Invent your own Keydown tag right inside a document! A line starting with `@style` defines it:

```
@style ~~sale~~ { background: linear-gradient(#ff8a00, #e52e71); color: #fff; border-radius: 999px; padding: 0 10px; }
```

Then write `~~sale~~text~~sale~~` anywhere after it, and it draws with that CSS: ~~sale~~50% off~~sale~~! The opening and closing marks can differ, like `@style <<star <<end { ... }`, and `&::before { ... }` / `&::after { ... }` inside the braces add a little shine or icon. The definition travels with the document (it saves back to its own `@style` line), so a style you invent is yours to keep, share, or put in an OpenDB lite.

### Live values

Write a little arithmetic right in the text, and it calculates:

* `{{price = 4.50}}` sets a value (it stays hidden while reading, and shows as a small chip while editing).
* `{{price * qty}}`, `{{money(price * qty, "$")}}`, `{{round(x, 2)}}` and more show a result.
* `{{today}}`, `{{days_until("2026-12-25")}}`, `{{year}}` read the date.
* `{{check paid}}`, `{{counter tickets = 2}}` and `{{slider temp = 27 from 20 to 34}}` are things you can click, and the totals around them update right away.

For example: {{price = 4.50}} {{qty = 3}} Tickets: {{qty}} × {{money(price, "$")}} = **{{money(price * qty, "$")}}**!

### Interactive blocks

A line from `::name` to `::end` becomes something you can use, right in the text:

* `::chart bar|line|area|pie|donut Title` then a `| table | of | numbers |` draws a chart.
* `::poll Question` then `* option` lines becomes something readers can vote on.
* `::quiz Question` then `* option` lines, with `* [x] option` marking the right answer.
* `::scene` holds a Zonerider scene (or a link to a `.zr`, `.glb` or `.gltf` file) and shows it in 3D.
* `::timer 5:00 Label` and `::map 38.72, -9.14 13 Lisbon` are one line each, no `::end` needed.

## Tables

Writeout and Bitbook have two ways to write a table.

* **Pipe tables**: one row per line, like `| Name | Score |`, with a line of `| --- | --- |` under the header row. Add a style after it, like `| --- | --- |{green}`.
* **Cell by cell**: `@@table=[a1h:'Name',b1:'Score',a2:'Ann',b2:'#O#9#']` names each cell like a spreadsheet (columns a to o, rows 1 and up) and gives its text in quotes. The text can use any Keydown, like chips and **bold**, and `\'` writes a quote. A style can follow the `]`, like `{green}`.

In a cell-by-cell table, an **h** after the cell name makes a header. A row is a header row when its first cell is a header (`a1h`), and then its whole row is headers. An h cell on any other row moves up to the nearest header row above it. Another header row further down starts a new part of the table:

@@table=[a1h:'Name',b1h:'Score',a2:'Ann',b2:'#O#9#',c3h:'Bonus',a4h:'Round 2',b4:'Score',a5:'Bob',b5:'**7**']{green}

## Files saved as Keydown

Writeout and Bitbook save their files (.kd and .kn) as Keydown text: a short header with the page size and other settings, then the document itself, readable in any text editor. When a file opens, the text turns back into formatting. A few extra spellings cover what the toolbar makes:

* **Colors, fonts and sizes**: `[words]{color=#dc2626}` colors the words, like [these words]{color=#dc2626}. `bg=` gives a background, like [a marker]{bg=#fde047}, `font="Georgia"` picks a font, `size=24px` a size and `weight=600` a weight. They can be combined: `[big blue words]{color=#2563eb size=20px weight=700}` gives [big blue words]{color=#2563eb size=20px weight=700}.
* **Alignment**: a line starting with `{align=center}` is centered; `right` and `justify` work too.
* **Pictures and files**: a picture you inserted is saved right in the file as `![name](data:image/png;base64,...)`, with `{width=240px}` after it when you resized it. An attached file is `[report.pdf](data:application/pdf;base64,...){file}`.
* **Tabs**: each tab of a document starts with a line like `::tab{name="Ideas"}`.
* **Plain symbols**: words that only look like Keydown keep their symbols as themselves, with a backslash, like `\#R\#not a highlight\#\#`.

## Code block styles
Open a fence with a language and a style in curly braces, and add a title after a bar!

```js{terminal|app.js}
npm run build
```

The styles are terminal, mac, dark, paper, blueprint, neon, ember, gel and diff. Add the word lines for line numbers, as in ```js{dark lines|app.js}. The diff style tints lines that start with + or -!

Inline code takes a style too, like `ls -la`{terminal}, `Cmd`{key} or `x`{neon}. The inline styles are terminal, dark, paper, blueprint, neon, ember, gel and key!

## Blank space for practice
`::blank{style|rows|Label}` leaves an empty box to write in, for textbooks and worksheets. The styles are box, dashed, lines, grid and dots. The rows are how tall it is (4 by default), and the label is optional. `::blank{6}` is a six-row box.

::blank{box|3|Try it yourself}

::blank{lines|4|Write your answer}

::blank{grid|4}

::blank{dots|3}

::blank{dashed|3|Sketch it here}

### Blanks inside a sentence
`[__8]` puts a blank to write in right inside a sentence, 8 characters wide. Leave the number out for a standard width. Add an answer after a bar, like `[__8|photosynthesis]`, and it stays hidden until you click the blank (it never prints).

Plants make food by [__14|photosynthesis] using [__6] and water.

`[##6]` is the same blank drawn as a small empty box instead of a line, and it takes an answer too, like `[##6|2024]`.

The war ended in [##6|1945] and began in [##6].

## Page number formats
`::pagenumbers{style|start}` is a small marker that changes how page numbers look from the page it sits on, until the next marker. The styles are `none` (front matter, no number), `arabic`, `roman` (i, ii, iii), `upper-roman` (I, II, III), `alpha` (a, b, c), `upper-alpha`, `greek` (α, β, γ) and `upper-greek`. The start number is optional and defaults to 1, so `::pagenumbers{roman|1}` starts a Roman section and `::pagenumbers{arabic|1}` starts the main text again at 1. Writeout and Bitbook show the numbers on their page sheets (turn on Page Numbers), and the marker itself never prints. In Writeout and Bitbook, use Layout > Pages > Page Number Format (or View in Bitbook) to place one at the caret!

## Poolside Keydown
**Poolside Keydown** is a dialect of Keydown written for Waterflow, with its own marks and forty original chips. Write it anywhere with the `;;pk{ }` block, for example ;;pk{<ring a ring chip>} inside a sentence. The full reference is in [[poolside-keydown]], and the look behind it is the [[poolside-style|Poolside style]].

